  "query_preview": "..\n     First measurable lowland snow of the season Sunday...\n\nSaturday and Sunday: Benign weather conditions will be in place\nthrough Saturday afternoon as a transient ridge slides through \nthe region in between systems. Areas of dense freezing fog are\nexpected to linger through Saturday morning as the weak late-\nNovember sun struggles to heat the ground and mix the moisture \naway from the surface. Morning low temperatures will be chilly\nin the mid 20s to low 30s.\n\nA shortwave swinging around the base of a deep offshore low will eject\nnortheastward through the Inland NW Saturday evening, sending a\nmoist occluding front across the region and bringing widespread\nlight snow. Snow will move into the Cascades and Central WA \nSaturday evening and expand to the east and northeast through \nSaturday night, reaching Northeast WA and the ID Panhandle by \nlate Sunday morning. Models continue to indicate a broad area of\nlift along the frontal boundary within the dendritic growth \nzone, which leads to moderate confidence in measurable \nQPF (greater than 0.01 inches) falling regionwide. Areas that \nhave the best chances of seeing higher QPF amounts of 0.10 \ninches or more include the upper Columbia Basin along the \nHighway 2 corridor, the Okanogan highlands and northern \nmountains, and the Central ID Panhandle where divergence aloft \nwill result in additional lift.\n\nIt's hard to say how much snow will actually accumulate,\nespecially for the eastern portions of the region including the\nSpokane/Coeur d'Alene area into the Central ID Panhandle, given\nground/soil temperatures still above freezing and dew points \nforecast to be between 33-35 degrees late Sunday morning into \nSunday afternoon when the bulk of the QPF is expected to fall. \nOverall, this is a tricky forecast, but it is possible we'll see\nthe first winter driving conditions of the season Saturday \nnight into Sunday. Confidence is moderate to high that impacts \nwill be minimal by Sunday afternoon.\n\nMonday: Ensemble clusters are in strong agreement on a shortwave\nridge passing through the region bringing dry conditions,\nseasonably cool temperatures in the mid 30s to low 40s, and a \nreturn of low clouds and fog. \n\nTuesday through Friday: An active pattern returns through this\nperiod with several systems expected to move through the region,\nbringing mountain snow and lowland rain and snow. Models are\ncoming into better agreement on the first of these systems\nmoving in Tuesday, bringing potential for several inches of snow\nover mountain passes. Details in exact snow amounts remain\nuncertain at this time, but prepare to encounter winter travel\nconditions if you have travel plans mid to late week. /Fewkes",
